\image(figures/buddy_allocator_order_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The lower curve combines three exactly equal measured counts; the upper curve has one extra successful order check. These are algorithm events, not timings or counts of ordered-set key comparisons.)
\alttext(As arena height grows from zero to thirty, allocation order checks grow from one to thirty-one. Splits, free buddy probes and merges are equal and grow from zero to thirty.)

The height bound alone does not prove O(log N) time for this implementation.
Its free sets and live maps are ordered associative containers. Membership,
insertion and keyed lookup have logarithmic container costs, as specified by
the [C++ associative-container requirements](https://eel.is/c++draft/associative.reqmts).
Writing H=h+1 for the number of orders and B for the current number of free
and live block records gives a conservative O(H log(B+1)) operation bound.
Constant-time buddy arithmetic does not make the membership lookup constant.
The usual O(H) buddy bound assumes suitable constant-time state lookup and
free-list operations; it should not be claimed here merely from the split count.

Metadata takes O(H+B) storage. Ordinary operations do not build trace vectors;
traced operations add O(H) records. Accounting methods scan live maps, while
a complete owned snapshot copies and sorts interval metadata, taking
O(B log(B+1)+H) time and O(B+H) storage. Snapshots are explicit operations,
not hidden work included in the order-count curves.

The constructor checks positive power-of-two dimensions and minimum<=total
before shifts or division. Requests must be positive. A positive request larger
than the arena safely returns minus one, with no eligible-order access; a
nonpositive request throws. Unknown frees retain their historical no-op policy.
A live reused offset identifies its current allocation, so this integer-offset
API cannot detect stale ownership from a previous allocation at that address.

Host allocation failure is distinct from insufficient logical arena capacity.
The core prepares new metadata before removing the old source or children,
and rolls allocation preparation back on failure. Thus a host exception leaves
the allocator state unchanged. An isolated native test injects failure at every
host-allocation position for plain/traced allocation and free, including split,
no-split, coalescing and blocked-buddy paths; it also verifies recovery after
each exception. Other native tests exhaust all 733 reachable eight-byte states
and 13,927 operation transitions against an independent occupancy bitmap.
Larger randomized arenas, original memory/format tests and UBSan check the
remaining arithmetic, ownership, accounting and trace claims.

> References

- K. C. Knowlton (1965), [A fast storage allocator](https://doi.org/10.1145/365628.365655),
  Communications of the ACM 8(10). The [author's bibliography](https://www.kenknowlton.com/pages/29papers.htm)
  identifies it as the binary splitting/recombination method later called the buddy system.
- Harvard CS 161 (2024), [Lecture 5: Buddy allocation, testing](https://read.seas.harvard.edu/cs161/2024/lectures/lecture5/),
  for aligned sibling coalescence and the data-structure assumptions behind height-based time bounds.
- C++ working draft, [Associative containers](https://eel.is/c++draft/associative.reqmts),
  for the ordered set/map operations used by this implementation.
